# Introducting::::Interdisciplinary Approaches to Perceptualizing Intangible Phenomena through Sound and Listening! 
## a month-long, student-led, research-intensive project by students from Newcastle University!

I am very pleased to have the opportunity to introduce a new student-led research intensive here at the Sonic Intangibles project. 
This project involves four students who will be introducing themselves to you here over the coming weeks. These students will be mentored
by members of our project team including myself (Jorge Boehringer) Dr. Chris Harrison, Rose Shepherd, and Dr. Bennett Hogg. This project 
resides in a nexus of the arts and sciences. The student practicioners have just finished their second year of study at the university.
Two are studying Physics with Astrophysics concentrations, one Music, and the other Fine Art. The staff mentors likewise represent a spectrum of 
expertise spanning physics, astrophysics, sound art, and music.

In brief, this project presents four students who have never worked together with the challenge and opportunity of designing and creating an 
interdisciplinary sonification of otherwise 'intangible' data or phenomena...in a very short but exceedingly focused period of time.
New skills will be learned and technologies developed. In fact, as the other of this first blog post, I am already learning a new skill which 
is posting a blog here on Github (a first for everything)!

Data sonification is the process of representing data with sound, which has many applications, including immersive communication of data, 
artistic works, technical data exploration, and as a means of amplifying accessibility in education. It is an alternative, or compliment to,
standard visual approaches but has struggled as a methodology to become mainstream and work effectively across disciplines. During the project, 
the team will co-design and develop sonification(s) from datasets related to phenomena that are “intangible”; for example, from sub-atomic particle
physics or from cosmology. The broad goal will be to make the “intangible” feel more “tangible” to audiences through the sonification.  The team will 
collectively define the core goals and output form of the final sonification piece.  However, the project is expected to result in a sonification
output in the form of recorded audio-(visual) piece and potentially a live performance of sound installation. A large part of the research project 
will evolve around understanding how the expertise across the different domains of art, music, and physics come together to approach this problem as an 
“interdiscipline”. Therefore, it will involve significant self-reflection, documenting of challenges, and sharing of solutions, for such an 
interdisciplinary team to work effectively.  The group will be mentored by an interdisciplinary team from the Newcastle-Northumbria Sonic Intangibles 
project (https://sonicintangibles.github.io/) and the results of this research scholarship will be embedded within this wider project’s goal of 
establishing how sonification can become an effective interdiscipline. In addition, the working processes of students and Sonic Intangibles project team members will be studied using ethno- and auto-ethnographic
methodologies, as we seek a deeper understanding of how interdisciplinary teams develop their functional working methodologies together.

I am very excited to get this project started and I know the students are too. Please keep your eyes peeled to towards this location for weekly updates!

